### Key Architectural Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| One DB per service | Strong isolation; schema changes in one service don't break others |
| RabbitMQ topic exchange | Flexible routing; multiple consumers can bind to same event without coupling |
| Durable queues + persistent messages | No message loss during RabbitMQ restarts or service downtime |
| Manual ACK + NACK + requeue | At-least-once delivery; failed processing is retried |
| Opaque refresh tokens (not JWT) | Refresh tokens can be invalidated server-side (JWTs cannot) |
| bcrypt cost=12 | ~250ms hash time — practical for login, exponentially expensive for brute force |
| SHA-256 hash of refresh tokens in DB | Stolen DB cannot be used to replay refresh tokens |
| Cloudinary for images, R2 for docs | Images need CDN + public URL; documents need private access + zero egress cost |
| Jitsi for video | Zero licensing cost, no vendor lock-in, browser-native WebRTC |
| PayHere for payments | Sri Lanka's leading payment gateway, supports LKR |
| Internal API Key for cross-service calls | Simpler than mutual TLS for same-network internal calls |
| EnsureProfile fallback in patient-service | Guards against missed RabbitMQ events — lazy creation from JWT |
| join_url hidden until paid | Prevents accessing telemedicine session without completing payment |
